From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: wize-logic <wizelogic@proton.me>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 20:11:59 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] recomp: --bounded-loops leaves RECOMP_BACKEDGE out of loops
 that end by themselves

RECOMP_BACKEDGE() before every backward jump gives a runtime a break-in
point in a loop that makes no call: a loop polling memory another thread
or an interrupt writes never ends without one. In a runtime that defines
it as a check of a flag, that is a load, a compare and a branch on every
iteration of every loop, and a counted loop or a scan to a bound pays it
for nothing: its own counter ends it whatever other threads do.

loop_bound.py finds those loops: one latch, no call, indirect jump,
interrupt, port, locked or halting instruction, and an exit test in the
header or the latch on a register the loop moves once per iteration by a
constant (inc, dec, add, sub, lea, loop), against an immediate, a register
the loop does not write, or a stack slot in a loop that stores nothing.
A test that goes on while the counter differs from the bound needs an odd
step. With --bounded-loops (off by default) their back edges are plain
gotos; every other back edge keeps the hook.
